.TH TBURN-CLI 1 "October 2026" "Tux Burner 0.99" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tburn-cli, tux-burner-cli \- command line of Tux Burner, GPU undervolting and tuning
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tburn-cli
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B tburn-cli \-\-amd\-status
.br
.B tburn-cli \-\-amd\-plan
.IR KEY=VALUE ...
[\fB\-\-amd\-apply\fR] [\fB\-\-amd\-dry\-run\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tux Burner
tunes GPU voltage, clocks, power limit and fans, and switches between saved
profiles while you play. On AMD GPUs it uses the amdgpu OverDrive sysfs interface;
on NVIDIA GPUs it uses the upstream NVML/NVAPI backend. Hardware writes are done by
the root service \fBtux-burnerd\fR(8); this command runs as your normal user and talks
to it over a local socket.
.PP
Tux Burner is a fork of PenguinBurner by jpietek
(https://github.com/jpietek/PenguinBurner), GPL-3.0-or-later. It is an independent
fork, not endorsed by or affiliated with the author of PenguinBurner.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the full list of options (it is long; the groups are Auto-UV, Steam overlay setup,
Runtime and daemon, Runtime tuning, Advanced/debug and AMD tuning).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and the fork notice, then exit.
.SS AMD (amdgpu) options
.TP
.B \-\-amd\-status
Print the GPU's OverDrive ranges, live values, fan curve and LACT state. Read-only.
.TP
.BI \-\-amd\-plan " KEY=VALUE..."
Describe a tuning plan. Keys: vo (VDDGFX offset in mV, negative = undervolt), sclk (core
clock offset in MHz), mclk (memory clock max in MHz), power (watts or a percentage such as 110%),
fan (up to five TEMP:PERCENT points). Without \fB\-\-amd\-apply\fR it only prints the writes.
.TP
.B \-\-amd\-dry\-run
With \fB\-\-amd\-apply\fR or \fB\-\-amd\-auto\-uv\fR: show what would be done, write nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-amd\-apply
Write the plan through tux-burnerd. Refused while LACT (lactd) is running unless
\fB\-\-override\-lact\fR is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-amd\-save\-profile " NAME"
Save the plan as a Tux Burner profile.
.TP
.BI \-\-amd\-profile\-tier " TIER"
Tier for the saved profile: efficiency, balanced or performance.
.TP
.BI \-\-amd\-export\-lact " FILE"
Write the plan as a LACT gpus: block.
.TP
.BI \-\-amd\-stress " SECONDS"
Run the OpenCL stress workload (sustained GPU load). No tuning writes.
.TP
.B \-\-amd\-auto\-uv
Run the VDDGFX undervolt ladder (sustained GPU load). Options: \fB\-\-amd\-auto\-uv\-start\fR,
\fB\-\-amd\-auto\-uv\-step\fR, \fB\-\-amd\-auto\-uv\-floor\fR, \fB\-\-amd\-auto\-uv\-rung\-seconds\fR,
\fB\-\-amd\-auto\-uv\-margin\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-amd\-voltage\-floor " MV"
Safety floor for the VDDGFX offset (default \-150). Going deeper than the default also needs
\fB\-\-allow\-below\-floor\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-below\-floor
Confirm that you want a floor deeper than the default.
.TP
.B \-\-override\-lact
Write even though LACT is running and owns the GPU.
.TP
.BI \-\-amd\-set\-daemon\-floor " MV|default"
Set the daemon's own floor (stored in /etc/tux-burner/amd-vddgfx-floor-mv).
.TP
.BR \-\-amd\-restore " {lact|stock}"
Restore the GPU: \fBlact\fR asks lactd to re-apply its profile; \fBstock\fR resets OverDrive, the
power cap and (without LACT) the fan curve to driver defaults through tux-burnerd.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/TuxBurner/
Saved profiles, run history, settings. Copied from ~/.config/PenguinBurner on first use.
.TP
.I /etc/tux-burner/amd-vddgfx-floor-mv
The daemon's VDDGFX safety floor.
.TP
.I /run/tux-burnerd.sock
The daemon socket.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tux-burner (1),
.BR tux-burnerd (8),
the user guide in /usr/share/doc/tux-burner/
